# Explore Singleton Avoidance and Triangular Growth

Begin with the apparent paradox: a rule advertised as anti-Fibonacci never grows. Move the slider in **The Rule That Always Returns Home** to see why one forbidden sum cannot dislodge the smallest positive integer.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The key mechanism is greedy selection: the forbidden sum of two positive terms is at least 2, so 1 remains available. [Greedy algorithms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greedy_algorithm) often depend more on which *small* candidates are blocked than on the magnitude of remote forbidden values.

<details><summary>Reveal the classification argument</summary>
If the predecessors are positive, their sum is at least 2. Thus 1 is an admissible positive integer, and no smaller positive integer exists. Every new term is therefore 1. Starting from 1,1, induction gives the unique constant trajectory. Consecutive ratios and greatest common divisors are both 1.
</details>

